CONTRIBUTING.md
     1→# Contributing to peepDB
     3→We're thrilled that you're interested in contributing to peepDB! This document provides guidelines for contributing to the project. Please take a moment to review this document to make the contribution process easy and effective for everyone involved.
     4→
     5→## Code of Conduct
     7→By participating in this project, you are expected to uphold our Code of Conduct (to be added).
     8→
     9→## Getting Started
    11→1. Fork the repository on GitHub.
    12→2. Clone your fork locally:
    13→   ```
    14→   git clone https://github.com/evangelosmeklis/peepdb.git
    15→   cd peepdb
    16→   ```
    17→3. Create a branch for your feature or bug fix:
    18→   ```
    19→   git checkout -b feature/your-feature-name
    20→   ```
    21→4. Make your changes and commit them with a clear commit message.
    22→5. Push your changes to your fork:
    23→   ```
    24→   git push origin feature/your-feature-name
    25→   ```
    26→6. Create a pull request from your fork to the main peepDB repository.
    27→
    28→## Setting Up Development Environment
    30→1. Ensure you have Python 3.6 or higher installed.
    31→2. Create a virtual environment:
    32→   ```
    33→   python -m venv venv
    34→   source venv/bin/activate  # On Windows: venv\Scripts\activate
    35→   ```
    36→3. Install the package in editable mode with development dependencies:
    37→   ```
    38→   pip install -e .[dev]
    39→   ```
    40→
    41→## Running Tests
    43→We use pytest for our test suite. To run the tests:
    44→
    45→```
    46→pytest
    47→```
    48→
    49→Please ensure all tests pass before submitting a pull request.
    50→
    51→## Coding Standards
    53→- Follow PEP 8 guidelines for Python code.
    54→- Use meaningful variable and function names.
    55→- Write docstrings for all functions, classes, and modules.
    56→- Keep lines to a maximum of 100 characters.
    57→- Use type hints where appropriate.
    58→
    59→## Pull Request Process
    61→1. Ensure your code adheres to the coding standards outlined above.
    62→2. Update the README.md with details of changes to the interface, if applicable.
    63→3. Add or update tests as necessary.
    64→4. Update the documentation to reflect any changes.
    65→5. Ensure all tests are passing.
    66→6. Your pull request will be reviewed by maintainers, who may request changes or provide feedback.
    67→
    68→## Reporting Bugs
    70→1. Check the GitHub Issues to see if the bug has already been reported.
    71→2. If not, create a new issue, providing as much detail as possible:
    72→   - A clear, descriptive title
    73→   - A detailed description of the issue
    74→   - Steps to reproduce the problem
    75→   - Expected behavior
    76→   - Actual behavior
    77→   - Your environment (OS, Python version, peepDB version, etc.)
    78→
    79→## Suggesting Enhancements
    81→We welcome suggestions for enhancements! Please create an issue on GitHub with the tag "enhancement" and provide:
    82→
    83→- A clear, descriptive title
    84→- A detailed description of the proposed enhancement
    85→- Any potential implementation deta

